<p>In the latest turn in the Iran war, President Trump said on March 30 that the United States could strike Iran&#x27;s oil and power infrastructure, including Kharg Island, if Tehran does not quickly accept terms to end the conflict. He also linked those threats to reopening the Strait of Hormuz. At the same time, the White House said peace contacts were continuing and &quot;progressing well,&quot; leaving a mix of coercive threats and diplomatic signals around the same talks. Iran, however, said it was not in direct negotiations with Washington and described proposals passed through intermediaries as excessive and unrealistic. Kharg Island is central to this escalation because it handles about 90% of Iran&#x27;s crude exports, making it a crucial source of state revenue and a clear pressure point in the war. Several reports say an attack there, or on other energy facilities, could also pull more oil out of already strained global markets, where prices have been rising sharply amid worries about shipping through the Strait of Hormuz. Even so, the continued talk of negotiations suggests diplomacy is still alive, offering at least a narrow path away from a wider regional and energy shock.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/GR3JIXAyBAtcamhEtV9MdlyTZMAm3AoKdXEqLEyESBr04FVH4tiOmwp1FdZiVGyfftsPCYDm6lNTYB-uGShmmsCFZuEaZ93uRHd_NEhgxIFHLza4Bs4LKKqUE2lw4Q30n-_aiypm7UFoTi3N7VXxJOYGHXj4-gmwmDK9GnM4x6ggKg9_K2zZIKjToNYsYtddyCohL-fxWFMDPz-F-0dEgoT3i8fys6G56p1Pql80xBQG9ELBWcNqbuSFCSUgQQb8JodsDCluO3g7' alt='A satellite image shows an oil terminal at Kharg Island, Iran, February 25, 2026.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Deadline whiplash: After delaying an earlier Iran deadline by 10 days, Trump quickly returned with a new public ultimatum, showing how fast the U.S. posture swung back toward escalation.</li><li>Civilian systems: The threatened target list went beyond oil facilities to electric plants and, in some reports, desalination or fresh-water infrastructure, raising the stakes for civilian services as well as energy output.</li><li>War messaging: President Trump also said a &quot;new, and more reasonable regime&quot; was in place and separately suggested &quot;regime change&quot; had already happened, while reporting from The New York Times said Iran&#x27;s power structure still appeared firmly in control.</li><li>Record-run risk: Reports said oil was on track for a record monthly rise, meaning the March 30 threat hit an already overheated energy market rather than creating a standalone shock.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>White House: The White House said peace talks with Iran were ongoing and progressing well, stressing that diplomatic contacts had continued despite the President&#x27;s threats. (<a href='http://www.euronews.com/2026/03/30/peace-talks-with-iran-ongoing-and-progressing-well-white-house-says'>Euronews</a>)</li><li>Iranian officials: Tehran said it was not in direct talks with Washington and called proposals passed through intermediaries excessive, unrealistic, and irrational. (<a href='https://www.thehindu.com/news/international/iran-israel-war-donald-trump-warns-tehran/article70802880.ece'>The Hindu</a>)</li><li>Market analysts cited by PBS: Analysts warned that knocking out Kharg Island would deprive Iran of revenue but also remove significant supply from world oil markets at a time of already soaring prices. (<a href='https://www.pbs.org/newshour/world/kharg-island-is-key-to-irans-oil-exports-targeting-it-carries-major-risks'>PBS NewsHour</a>)</li><li>New York Times analysis: The New York Times reported that despite President Trump&#x27;s statements about political change, Iran&#x27;s leadership structure appeared to remain firmly in control after a month of US-Israeli attacks. (<a href='https://www.nytimes.com/2026/03/30/world/middleeast/trump-regime-change-iran.html'>The New York Times</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.lemonde.fr/en/international/article/2026/03/30/trump-